>> It seems that it would make more sense if potions of oil didn't dilute
>> in water.  Is there a reason this not to be the case?

The dilution in Nethack works different from Real Life. A second
dip will make (most) potions pure water, so we may assume than we
have replaced (not strictly diluted) the contents of the phial.

> Have you never made a salad dressing? Oil and water don't mix per se, 
> but if you agitate them enough you get an emulsion where oil and water 
> is unseparable (1).

Yes, in short term; most substances auto-separate after a while.

> (1) With normal kitchen implements you don't get such emulsion that it 
> wont separate if you let it stay for months.

Separation time depends on many factors, the most effective is
the non-diluting component's difference in their specific weight
("specific gravity"?), another one is the size of the droplets in
the emulsion.

